1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:07,563
In 1986, workers near the Black Sea broke into a cave that had been sealed for five and a half million years.

2
00:00:07,513 --> 00:00:13,790
Inside: toxic air, total darkness, and thirty-five species found nowhere else on Earth.

3
00:00:13,790 --> 00:00:16,847
Oxygen at just seven percent.

4
00:00:16,847 --> 00:00:19,959
Air thick with hydrogen sulfide.

5
00:00:19,959 --> 00:00:27,894
And yet life is everywhere: blind spiders, eyeless leeches, ghost-pale centipedes, none of them needing sunlight to survive.

6
00:00:27,894 --> 00:00:30,543
And this cave is not alone.

7
00:00:30,543 --> 00:00:43,777
In the deepest caves on Earth, two kilometers down, a springtail called Plutomurus holds the record for the deepest animal ever found: 1,980 meters below the surface, in a world with no light at all.

8
00:00:43,777 --> 00:00:46,739
These creatures do not eat sunlight.

9
00:00:46,739 --> 00:00:54,130
They eat chemistry: bacteria turning minerals and sulfur into food, exactly like the vents of the deep ocean.

10
00:00:54,130 --> 00:00:57,948
Entire ecosystems running on nothing but rock.

11
00:00:57,948 --> 00:01:05,896
So next time you stand in the sun, remember: two kilometers below your feet, life is thriving in a world that has never seen it.

12
00:01:05,896 --> 00:01:09,402
Follow Depth Unknown for more from the dark.

